ret += hh * usPerHr;
ret += mm * usPerMin;
ret += ss * usPerSec;
return ret;
Reason: The values hh, mm, ss (and us) are converted into microsecond units using constants like usPerHr, usPerMin, usPerSec, but the resulting value `ret` is returned directly without converting microseconds back to seconds. Since the rest of the application expects time values in seconds, this causes the returned time to be off by a factor of 1,000,000.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
return ret * 1e-6;
return ret * 1e-6;
Reason: Multiplying the microsecond-based total `ret` by 1e-6 converts it into seconds, correctly aligning the parsed time value with the units expected by the rest of the application, thus fixing the factor-of-1,000,000 discrepancy.

